cabulary and why do i think this is a  better way to learn languages i have  five reasons first of all because it  works it works very well some of the  best polyglots on the internet or the  famous cattle lomb  if you study their methods you'll see  that it generally involves a lot of  reading 100 years or so 150 years ago  and of course today with the  availability of new technology listening  so and in my own case it's worked for me  and my most recent experience was czech  where for the first eight months i only  listened and read and then gradually  started speaking and stepped up speaking  uh just prior to going to prague and i  arrived in prague and i could understand  everything and my speaking uh which was  already at a call at an intermediate you  know low intermediate level i think i  stepped up to maybe a  middle intermediate level and of course  you have to continue speaking but i was  able to do all that because i had a  sound basis in the language and that  sound basis comes from a lot of  listening reading so  it's effective number one number two  it's easy you can do it anywhere you can  do it listening in your car washing the  dishes as i do or exercising and you  just have it with you you're waiting  somewhere you do it so it's very easy  similarly with reading particularly now  i didn't mention it in chinese or  japanese but we have our ipad app so if  you're doing it  uh at link or somewhere else you can  have  reading on your ipad you can print stuff  and read it you can borrow stuff from  the library you can do it it's so  flexible you don't have to go to a  classroom half an hour to get there sit  in the class half an hour to come back  uh  also in terms of effectiveness uh i just  wanted to mention too like if you're  listening or reading you're you're a  hundred percent with the language in a  classroom half the time you're having to  listen to other students who may not use  the language as well as you do  and so it's to my mind much less  effective than time you spend with the  language the third thing about input  based learning is you're not making  mistakes a lot of people are afraid to  make mistakes if you're forced to speak  you'll make mistakes  uh  you're listening you can't make a  mistake when you're listening you might  misunderstand something you might not it  might be a little fuzzy at times you may  have the wrong interpretation there's  words you don't understand none of that  matters that's part of the process  that's part of the process  and things that are unclear and fuzzy at  an early stage will eventually start to  become clearer so you're not really  making mistakes but you're in that stage  of your learning where the brain is  gradually becoming more and more  familiar with the language you're  learning more and more words and of  course things are going to be unclear to  you  so that's an advantage another advantage  of listening and reading is that you can  do things that are interesting because  you can choose what you want to listen  to and read  obviously the first month or so you're  stuck with beginner material which is  often not very interesting but i  certainly encourage people to move  beyond the beginner material as soon as  possible to get into things of interest  because i certainly find that if i find  something of interest i will work even  if there's a lot of unknown words i'll  work hard with that text because it's of  interest to me  so you can be doing stuff that's of  interest and in the case of my tech  learning  my russian before that i was able to  learn so much about czech history the  history of central europe uh political  situation in czech  czech  republic  so when i get to prague i have all this  wonderful background so you're doing  stuff that's interesting you're learning  other things  besides just the language itself which  is more interesting than sitting in a  classroom again because they're  necessarily you're dealing with learner  material that's been devised by someone  to make sure it includes all of this  vocabulary and this grammar structure  and stuff which which often makes it  very uninteresting  and finally learning uh via an input  based approach  is cheaper because you can you don't  have to spend anything you can go to the  library you know you can find stuff on  the internet if you want to uh there are  services like link or you can buy  yourself an assy mail or you can buy  yourself a pimsleur whatever  but it's still a lot less expensive than  going to class  so now it may be that someone else is  paying for the class but that doesn't  change the fact that the class is  expensive inherently because you have a  trained professional there in front of  uh students someone's got to pay for him  i'm not i'm not saying you shouldn't go  to class  but  even if you do the main emphasis in my  opinion should be on input based  learning now input based learning has  a drawback  and that is that for it to be effective  you have to be motivated you have to be  disciplined you have to be a  self-starter  uh you have to be curious about things  you have to go out and find content of  interest  you have to have the confidence that you  can succeed and this is often the  problem with with inexperienced lions  learners who have never really become  fluent in another language they can't  visualize themselves as fluent so  they're kind of half defeated before  they start they think they'll never get  there and if you think that then  probably you won't so you have to be a  positive confident  motivated  independent learner  however  if you go to a class and you aren't a  confident motivated independent learner  you won't either you won't learn either  and  i i mentioned the chinese  video that i did that they did a study  of chinese immigrants to canada and  found that in seven years they followed  about 3 000 immigrants who were taking  esl  here at these government-sponsored  schools and there was essentially no  improvement statistically no improvement  uh those that spoke well when they  arrived spoke well continue to speak  well and those that didn't speak when  they arrived still couldn't speak very  well so  that's just to say that very often the  classroom environment if the learner is  not motivated in other words doesn't  have all the qualities that you require  to be an independent learner and to take  advantage of listening and reading and  input based learning if you don't have  those qualities you won't be successful  in the classroom either so the classroom  can provide a lot of benefits social  benefits and feedback and so forth but  even if you're in a classroom  make sure that your main emphasis is on  listening and reading and building up  your vocabulary and there you have it  i'm sure that i'll get a lot of flack  back for this one but i really strongly  believe it it's it's the thing that  people don't understand you don't have  to speak from day one you don't have to  do drills you have to listen and read a  lot thank you for listening
 

